    "content": "El Niño is a climate pattern marked by warmer-than-average sea surface temperatures in the equatorial Pacific Ocean. It typically occurs every 2-7 years, often bringing wetter, cooler conditions to the Southern US and warmer, drier conditions to the North. A \"Super El Niño\" in 2026 may cause stronger global impacts. Key Impacts of El Niño Global Temperature and Weather: Shifts the Pacific jet stream southward, which causes significant changes in global weather patterns. Wetter Conditions: Enhanced storm activity and increased precipitation often occur in the southern US, Peru, Ecuador, and parts of the Caribbean. Drier Conditions: Drought and extreme heat often strike Indonesia, Australia, and parts of Africa. North American Winter: The southern tier of the U.S. experiences wetter and colder conditions, while northern U.S. states and Canada tend to be warmer and drier than average. Hurricane Activity: Generally increases Pacific hurricane activity while weakening Atlantic hurricanes, according to. Marine Life: Decreases nutrient-rich upwelling along the Pacific coast of South America, which can devastate local fishing industries. 2026 ForecastForecasters are monitoring the development of a potential \"Super El Niño\" for 2026, which could be one of the strongest in a century. This type of event is defined by the ocean temperatures warming more than 2∘𝐶 (3.6∘𝐹) above average, with peak impacts usually occurring between December and January.",
